data: {"type":"response.reasoning_summary_text.done","summary_index":0,"item_id":"rs_02179466216066400000000000000000000ffffac155396dace9d","output_index":0,"text":"Formal verifier wants unconditional once per scan calls. Our conditional double-calls cause unsupported. Need refactor each timer called unconditionally once per scan. We can preserve semantics by calls once, but initialization on transitions via a helper? TON only call once. Need structure.\n\nWe can call each timer unconditionally once per scan with IN based on prior/current states and inspect Q. Need get exact timing incl first energized sample elapsed 0, cooldown clear at 3s same scan, same-scan restart. Could use internal flags / timestamps, maybe avoid TON and implement timer via TIME? Formal may not support time functions. Could use explicit integer accumulators advanced by `dt_ms`, but no dt input. However simulation harness may expose current task cycle time? Perhaps use `TON` unconditionally with IN pins only and still get behavior by ordering.\n\nLet's analyze unconditional TON semantics. Call once at start or end. We can call both timers at start with IN based on previous outputs. Then decide. For cooldown, if trip this scan, start call was false, so cooldown doesn't initialize until next scan -> one late. Could initialize by designing cooldown timer to run based on `OverheatProtection OR (heatLimitReached)` at start? heatLimitReached known from heat timer call. But at start if no protection previous and heat reaches limit this scan, we can pass IN := (wasProtected OR heatLimitReached) to cooldown timer at same scan, once. It gets rising edge true, elapsed 0. Then set protection. Good. For active scans, pass true. So:\n```\nheatTimer(IN := wasHeating AND NOT sensorFault, PT:=5s);\nheatLimitReached := heatTimer.Q AND NOT sensorFault;\ncooldownTimer(IN := wasProtected OR heatLimitReached, PT:=3s);\nIF cooldownTimer.Q AND wasProtected THEN clear...\nIF heatLimitReached AND NOT wasProtected THEN trip...\n```\nAt trip scan, cooldown called with IN true (because heatLimitReached) starting elapsed0, after setting protection. Next scan true, adds dt. At +3s, when cooldownTimer.Q true, if wasProtected true, clear. Since call once at start, Q observed same scan. Great. No second call.\nAt clear scan, call cooldown at start IN:=wasProtected true, adds dt, Q true, clear. No end call; next scan start sees wasProtected false (since output set false) -> cooldown IN false resets. Fine. But then if protection clears and restarts same scan, we want heatTimer initialized at current scan. heatTimer call at start used wasHeating (previous false) => reset. But no call at end with new HeaterOutput true. Next scan start heatTimer IN true. As discussed, TON sees rising edge and may report elapsed 0, meaning first energized scan was actually previous scan but timer didn't know. At next scan, continuous heat time should be dt not 0. One-scan late trip. Need initialize heat timer same scan.\n\nCould arrange heatTimer call once per scan at end, after output decision, with IN = HeaterOutput, then inspect Q after call to trip in same scan. But need reset/trip and final output. We can use current scan's tentative output, call heatTimer, then if Q trip and override. One call. Let's explore:\n\nAt scan:\n- Compute demand/fault.\n- Determine whether previous protection active and cooldown clears. Cooldown must be called once at start, but trip initialization issue could be solved by calling cooldown after trip decision and then checking Q? Call cooldown once after protection state resolved. On trip scan, call after setting protection true -> elapsed0. On active scans, call with protection true -> dt and Q. If Q true at start? After call, if previously active and Q true, clear before output. But call happens after? We can call cooldown after tentatively deciding protection from previous. Then if Q and wasProtected, clear, then recompute output. On trip, call with true -> Q false. Good. Ordering:\n1. Determine provisional protection: `nextProtect := OverheatProtection` (prior). If prior active, remains for now. If no prior and need trip? Need heatLimit first, which needs heat timer call.\n2. Handle heatTimer call based on previous/current output? Could call at end with tentative output. But trip detection same scan.\nMaybe call heat timer around output:\n- provisional output considering prior protection/demand/fault.\n- heatTimer(IN := provisional output AND NOT sensorFault, PT5)\n- heatLimitReached := heatTimer.Q.\n- if Q then OverheatProtection true, output false.\n- cooldownTimer(IN := OverheatProtection, PT3) after final protection decision; if wasProtected and Q then clear protection (but Q computed based perhaps true previous scan). However call with IN true at trip scan first eligible 0. Active scan at +3 returns Q true and can clear same scan. If clear, output could restart same scan but heatTimer already called with false/provisional false; no re-call. We can adjust output to true, heatTimer remains reset, and heat timer next scan rising edge issue again. But if heatTimer call happened at scan of restart with final true? It was called with provisional output during protection false (since prior protection true) => false. After cooldown clears same scan, output true but timer reset. Next scan call true and TON first eligible elapsed 0 -> delayed. Need somehow heat timer initialization same scan without second call.\n\nCan choose call heat timer after cooldown resolution and after determining final output except for heat trip. Circular: heat trip uses timer, final output uses trip, timer input uses final output. But we can model with logic: Determine output candidate based on prior protection, demand, fault. Call heatTimer(IN := output candidate). If Q -> trip. Then call cooldownTimer after final protection (once). If cooldown was active and Q -> clear and restart same scan. In restart case, heatTimer input should have been true but was false due prior protection. Could modify heatTimer's IN to include \"will restart due cooldown done\" before calling: compute cooldownDone flag from prior state? `cooldownTimer` must be called to know Q, but we could call cooldown first based on protection previous, get Q. Then if Q and protection prior, clear. Then compute output including restarted heat; then call heat timer with final provisional output. If heat Q, trip and output false; now cooldown timer was called under prior protection true but Q true, we clear. If then heat trips immediately (unlikely on restart first scan because heatTimer reset? But if heatTimer call input true and previous call was false during protection, TON rising edge gives elapsed0, so no trip), protection false. Fine.\nOn trip scan when prior protection false: call cooldown first with IN := wasProtected (false) resets. Then compute output based demand true. Call heatTimer(IN true) adds dt (previous timer had true from last scan if output true) and Q true -> trip; set protection true. But cooldown timer was called earlier false, so not initialized. Need call cooldown again or use IN for cooldown including predicted heatLimitReached, but heatLimit known only after heat timer. Could decide trip based on heatTimer.Q and then... hmm.\n\nCould call cooldown first with `IN := wasProtected OR predictedHeatLimit` where predictedHeatLimit could be `heatTimer.Q` from previous scan? The threshold crossing this scan is known from prior timer state plus dt but without call. Maybe use `heatTimer.ET`? CODESYS TON exposes ET? Yes TON has `ET : TIME`; but Q from last call. At start of scan, before call, ET from previous scan; can't know dt. Could use `heatTimer.Q` previous (false before crossing). No.\n\nCould call heatTimer first, then cooldown, then if trip after heatTimer, cooldown sees `IN := wasProtected OR heatLimitReached` and initializes. Then compute output. This seems good. Cooldown is called after heatTimer and before output. Cooldown Q can clear prior protection. Then output decision may restart. But heatTimer was already called with what input? We need heatTimer input to represent heating across current interval based on prior output, not current output. We can use `wasHeating AND NOT sensorFault` for heatTimer. That handles continuous time from previous energized scan. It doesn't initialize when output starts this scan. Could initialize same scan by adding a second call—not allowed. But perhaps TON rising edge next scan semantics actually elapsed = dt since previous invocation, not 0? Need know CODESYS TON semantics. Standard TON ET on rising edge might indeed be 0. Let's verify via simulation. We can design test. We have 3 sim. Need understand output around thresholds.\n\nAlternative implement our own timers with TIME arithmetic and `LTIME`? Formal verifier supports synchronous state machine, probably not TIME calls. But maybe simulation uses dt. Could use `TON` unconditionally but call once per scan at end and structure to init. Let's derive a scheme with one call each and no delayed semantics by treating \"eligible first energized scan\" as the scan after previous output was true? Wait evaluation likely compares expected based on output observed and dt. If our trip occurs one scan late, tests fail.\n\nLet's understand actual TON in simulation: likely the harness has custom timer model that advances TON based on dt_ms. It may emulate CODESYS TON semantics: call with IN true at first scan after output turned true; since dt_ms is elapsed since previous scan and timer was reset last scan, perhaps it adds dt. The task note \"Timer elapsed time is zero on the first eligible sampled invocation; dt_ms is elapsed wall time since the previous invocation\". This might be generic evaluator model where any timer called with IN true adds dt except first eligible zero. In implementation, first eligible is when timer call sees IN true. If that's scan1 (output became true scan0), dt since previous invocation? At scan0 timer call was IN false; at scan1 first eligible, the spec says elapsed zero, so expected trip shifts. But expected trace probably assumes timer starts at scan0 when heater energized. They state first energized sample elapsed zero. So our implementation must invoke timer with IN true at scan0 to be aligned.\n\nWe can call timers unconditionally once per scan after output decision. Then detect Q after call same scan and override output if Q. For cooldown, call after protection decision and detect Q same call. That gives correct. Let's attempt formal-friendly structure with one call each per scan, and handle circular by tentative output.\n\nMaybe explicit state machine with two accumulators manually based on `TIME`? Hmm.\n\nLet's design with heat timer called once after computing a \"requested output\", then adjust:\n1. Compute fault/demand.\n2. Compute preliminary protection clear: Need cooldown timer. Let's call cooldown after heat timer? Could call cooldown once at end after final protection. We need if active and cooldownDone to clear same scan before output.\nWe can call cooldown at start unconditionally once:\n```\ncooldownTimer(IN := OverheatProtection, PT:=3s);\nIF OverheatProtection AND cooldownTimer.Q THEN\n    OverheatProtection := FALSE;\nEND_IF;\n```\nThis call uses prior OverheatProtection, clears when Q. On trip later this scan, no initialization. Could adjust by on trip set a custom flag `cooldownStart` and next scan? Instead we can initialize cooldown timer by controlling `IN` to include future trip predicted from heatTimer output, but need order.\n\nOrder:\na. Call heatTimer first with IN := HeaterOutput? But before output decision, previous output. Gives heatLimitReached threshold crossing this scan. Also on restart from cooldown same scan, we need later output. We can call heatTimer at end instead. Circular only trip and clear.\nCould call heatTimer with IN := `(demand AND NOT sensorFault AND NOT OverheatProtection AND NOT wasProtected?)` candidate output, but if cooldown clears this scan, OverheatProtection still true before clear. Use wasProtected and cooldown Q... circular cooldown call.\nCould use two-phase but single invocation each with \"tentative\" values:\n- Call cooldown first with `IN := wasProtected OR heatLimitReached_prev_scan?` Not enough.\nMaybe use timer ET directly to determine cooldownDone without call? At scan K, cooldownTimer.ET from previous invocation. If at previous scan ET < 3 and dt unknown. Could use Q only.\n\nAlternative don't use timers for cooldown; use an integer counter incremented each scan? But dt not uniform. Tests might use variable dt. TON needed.\n\nCould use one `TON` for cooldown called unconditionally at end, and on trip call it with IN true; Q observed on subsequent scans. For clear same scan, after call check Q and clear same scan, recompute output. This is one call. Ordering:\n1. Compute fault/demand.\n2. Compute tentative protection state from prior `OverheatProtection` and maybe heat limit.\n3. Compute tentative output.\n4. Call heatTimer(IN := tentative output AND NOT fault? or actual intended) once.\n5. If heatTimer.Q trip -> protection true, output false.\n6. Call cooldownTimer(IN := protection).\n7. If protection and cooldownTimer.Q -> clear protection, and recompute possible output true.\nBut if step 7 restarts, heatTimer was called with false; not initialized. Could after step 7 not call again. But next scan first eligible issue. Maybe acceptable if we handle heat timing based on prior output at next scan: when heater became true at scan k, at scan k+1 TON sees IN true after being reset false. If rising edge elapsed 0 then trip at k+6 not k+5. Could compensate by passing `IN := wasHeating OR HeaterOutput`? Let's test actual TON semantics with simulate.\n\nCan simulate candidate with cases and expected maybe tool doesn't check expected? It returns outputs. Let's learn schema. Use a simple case. We need 3 sim calls max. Let's call with steps dt_ms. We can pass expected approximate. Need know output format. Try:\n```\n{\"cases\":[{\"name\":\"trip\",\"steps\":[{\"inputs\":{...},\"dt_ms\":1000,\"expected\":{...}}]}]}\n```\nNo need expected? We'll include. Maybe schema uses `expected_outputs`, `step_ms`. The tool may tell. We have budget. But before that, let's inspect tool docs impossible. We can infer from description \"List of test cases with steps (inputs, expected, optional dt_ms)\". So steps objects inputs, expected, dt_ms. Cases likely name. Use.\n\nLet's simulate current candidate with 10 steps: temp 20 set 30 from scan0 through maybe scan8. Need observe trip timing. Expected according semantic:\nscan0 dt0 ignored? first scan output true, no overheat.\nscan1 dt1000 -> output true\nscan2 2000\nscan3 3000\nscan4 4000\nscan5 5000 -> trip protection true output false\nscan6 1000 cooldown (trip at t5, t6=+1) protection true\nscan7 +2\nscan8 +3 -> clear, demand true => output true same scan, protection false\nscan9 heat restarts first sample (output true from scan8; actually continuous heater energized at scan8 elapsed0, scan9 elapsed1)\nLet's simulate and see. Expected in each step? Simulation likely compares expected. We'll set expected according. This uses one sim call. But 3 max. Fine.\n\nCurrent candidate call ordering:\n- scan0: heatTimer start false; cooldown false; output true; heatTimer end true (call true) => likely 0.\n- scan1: heatTimer start true adds 1; output true no end.\n...\n- scan5 start true adds 5 -> Q -> protection true, cooldown at active call start false then end true; output false.\n- scan6 start heat false resets; cooldown true adds 1. \n- scan7 adds 2.\n- scan8 adds 3 Q -> clear; output true; heatTimer start false, end true init.\nWorks if TON first call logic.\n\nLet's simulate.","sequence_number":1277}

